window.MTH105_INDEX=[{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#statistics","n":"What is statistics?","t":"What is statistics?","x":"Statistics is the science of collecting, organizing, analyzing, interpreting and communicating data to support decisions. A transport authority wants to know whether a new Metro feeder bus is worth running. It counts riders (collect), puts them in a table (organize), draws a chart (analyze), reads it (interpret) and reports a decision (communicate). Data: illustrative. Every statistics question follows the same five steps. This course teaches each one."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#descriptive","n":"Descriptive and inferential statistics","t":"Descriptive and inferential statistics","x":"Descriptive statistics describe the data we have: tables, graphs, averages. Inferential statistics use a sample to say something about the whole population. Sort each statement. Ask: does it stay with the data we have, or go beyond it? Weeks 1–6 are mostly descriptive. From Week 10, we use samples to estimate the population: inferential statistics."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#population","n":"Population and sample","t":"Population and sample","x":"The population is the whole group we study (size N). A sample is the part we measure (size n). A number describing the population is a parameter; a number from a sample is a statistic. A survey of 250 Dubai residents finds that 85 use the Metro. The 250 are the sample; all Dubai residents are the population. Data: illustrative. We use the statistic (p̂ = 34%) to estimate the unknown parameter p: the true share of all residents."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#types","n":"Types of data","t":"Types of data","x":"Qualitative data are categories: nominal (no order) or ordinal (ordered). Quantitative data are numbers: discrete (counted) or continuous (measured). Classify these everyday variables. The type decides the tools: categories → counts, bar and pie charts, mode. Numbers → histograms, mean, spread."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#samples","n":"Representative samples and sample size","t":"Representative samples and sample size","x":"A representative sample looks like the population. Choose it at random, not by convenience. Larger random samples give estimates closer to the truth. A newsletter asks 30 students in the library at 9 PM; 21 say they prefer studying at night. Headline: “70% of students prefer night study.” The sample is biased: students in the library at night already study at night. Drag the sample size. With small n, sample results swing widely; with large n, they cluster near the true value."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#outliers","n":"The impact of outliers","t":"The impact of outliers","x":"An outlier is a value far from the rest of the data. Pizza delivery times (min): 12, 14, 13, 15, 46. One late delivery (46) is far from the others. The outlier pulls the mean up to 20; the median stays at 14. With outliers, report the median."},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#in-class","n":"In class","t":"In class","x":""},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#takeaways","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":""},{"pt":"Week 1","l":"week-01.html#practice","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Solve on paper first, then open the model working. Easy to hard."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#frequency","n":"Frequency and relative frequency","t":"Frequency and relative frequency","x":"The frequency f is how many times a category occurs. The relative frequency rf is its share of the total: f ÷ n. 40 students record how they get to campus: car 16, Metro 12, bus 8, walk 4. Data: illustrative. For categories, a bar chart compares sizes; a pie chart shows parts of a whole (100%)."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#classes","n":"Grouping numbers into classes","t":"Grouping numbers into classes","x":"Many numbers are grouped into classes of equal width. Each class has limits, boundaries (± 0.5) and a midpoint. 25 test scores range from 42 to 98. Listing all 25 shows little; six classes show the pattern. Build the frequency table: class, boundaries, midpoint, f, rf and cumulative frequency. Type your own data."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#histogram","n":"Histograms and shape","t":"Histograms and shape","x":"A histogram draws a frequency table as touching bars, one per class. Its shape is symmetric, right-skewed or left-skewed. Ages of 30 visitors to a science museum: most are 10–39, a few are older. Data: illustrative. Follow the tail. Tail to the right: positively skewed. Tail to the left: negatively skewed. No long tail: symmetric."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#polygon","n":"Frequency polygon and ogive","t":"Frequency polygon and ogive","x":"A frequency polygon joins the class midpoints. An ogive plots cumulative frequency at each upper boundary. From the museum data: how many visitors were younger than 49.5? The ogive answers directly: 26 of 30. The polygon shows the shape. The ogive answers “how many below a value?”."},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#in-class","n":"In class","t":"In class","x":""},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#takeaways","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":""},{"pt":"Week 2","l":"week-02.html#practice","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Solve on paper first, then open the model working. Easy to hard."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#mean","n":"The mean","t":"The mean","x":"The mean x̄ is the balance point of the data: add all values and divide by how many there are. Five friends sleep 8, 9, 10, 12 and 13 hours over a weekend. What is the typical amount? Data: illustrative. If all five slept the same amount, each would sleep 10.4 hours. The mean uses every value, so one extreme value moves it."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#median","n":"Median and mode","t":"Median and mode","x":"The median is the middle value after sorting. The mode is the most frequent value; it is the only centre for nominal data. Delivery times (min): 7, 3, 9, 5, 20, 6. Sort first: 3, 5, 6, 7, 9, 20. Type any data to see the middle and the mode."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#which","n":"Which centre to use?","t":"Which centre to use?","x":"In symmetric data, mean ≈ median ≈ mode. Skew pulls the mean towards the tail. Salaries in a small company: most earn 42–62 thousand, three managers earn 150–200 thousand. Data: Week 1–2 slides. For skewed data or outliers, report the median. For symmetric data, the mean."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#quartiles","n":"Percentiles and quartiles","t":"Percentiles and quartiles","x":"A percentile gives the position of a value: P25 = Q1, P50 = median, P75 = Q3. “Your score is at the 75th percentile” means 75% of the class scored at or below you. Sort, find the median, then the median of each half. When n is odd, leave the median out of both halves."},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#in-class","n":"In class","t":"In class","x":""},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#takeaways","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":""},{"pt":"Week 3","l":"week-03.html#practice","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Solve on paper first, then open the model working. Easy to hard."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#range-iqr","n":"Range, IQR and the boxplot","t":"Range, IQR and the boxplot","x":"The range is max − min. The IQR (interquartile range) is Q3 − Q1: the spread of the middle 50%. Two bus routes both average 30 minutes. On one, trips take 28–32 min; on the other, 15–60 min. Same mean, very different reliability. Data: illustrative. The boxplot draws the five-number summary: min, Q1, median, Q3, max. A long whisker or a dot beyond the fences shows skew or outliers."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#variance","n":"Sample variance","t":"Sample variance","x":"The variance s² is the average squared distance from the mean. For a sample, divide by n − 1. Five students’ quiz scores: 4, 6, 7, 9, 14. Mean = 8. How far are they from 8, on average? Variance is in squared units (points²), so it is hard to read. That is why we take its square root next."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#sd","n":"Standard deviation","t":"Standard deviation","x":"The standard deviation s is the square root of the variance: the typical distance from the mean, in the original units. Same quiz scores: s² = 14.5 points². A typical score is about 3.8 points from the mean of 8. Larger s = more spread. Check any data set step by step with the deviation table."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#cv","n":"Coefficient of variation","t":"Coefficient of variation","x":"The coefficient of variation (CV) is the standard deviation as a percentage of the mean. City A temperature: mean 15 °C, s 6 °C. City B rainfall: mean 100 mm, s 30 mm. Different units: which varies more? Data: illustrative. CV has no units, so it compares spread across different means or units. Smaller CV = more consistent. Here temperature varies more, relative to its mean."},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#in-class","n":"In class","t":"In class","x":""},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#takeaways","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":""},{"pt":"Week 4","l":"week-04.html#practice","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Solve on paper first, then open the model working. Easy to hard."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#sample-space","n":"Sample space and events","t":"Sample space and events","x":"An experiment has uncertain results; each result is an outcome. All outcomes form the sample space S. An event is a set of outcomes. Roll one die: S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}. “Even number” is the event {2, 4, 6}. Before calculating any probability, list S and the outcomes in the event."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#basic-rule","n":"The basic rule","t":"The basic rule","x":"When outcomes are equally likely, P(E) = favourable outcomes ÷ total outcomes. Every probability is between 0 and 1. A bag holds 5 red, 3 blue and 2 green balls. Pick one without looking. Data: illustrative. 0.5 means red comes up in about half of many repeated picks. All outcomes together add up to 1."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#complement","n":"The complement rule","t":"The complement rule","x":"“Not A” is everything outside A. P(A) and P(not A) always add to 1. 20% of deliveries arrive late. What share arrives on time? Data: illustrative. Use the complement when “not A” or “at least one” is easier to count the other way round."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#addition","n":"The addition rule (OR)","t":"The addition rule (OR)","x":"“A or B” means at least one happens. Add, then subtract the overlap so it is not counted twice. If A and B are mutually exclusive (no overlap), just add. Of 60 students, 35 take Arabic, 25 take French and 10 take both. Data: illustrative. In a Venn diagram, the overlap sits inside both circles. Change the counts and watch the rule."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#multiplication","n":"The multiplication rule (AND) and tree diagrams","t":"The multiplication rule (AND) and tree diagrams","x":"For independent events, P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B). In a tree, multiply along a path; add paths for “or”. A student is assigned a group (Male or Female) and a lab session (Morning or Evening) at random. Each split is 1/2. The tree lists the sample space: 2 × 2 = 4 equally likely paths. P(Evening) = 1/4 + 1/4 = 1/2 (add the paths)."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#event-types","n":"Types of events","t":"Types of events","x":"Mutually exclusive: cannot happen together. Independent: one does not change the other. Dependent: one changes the other. Complementary: opposites that add to 1. Name the relationship for each pair. The relationship decides the rule: OR → add (minus the overlap); AND → multiply; NOT → 1 minus."},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#in-class","n":"In class","t":"In class","x":""},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#takeaways","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":""},{"pt":"Week 5","l":"week-05.html#practice","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Solve on paper first, then open the model working. Easy to hard."},{"pt":"Week 6","l":"week-06.html#random-variable","n":"Discrete random variables and valid distributions","t":"Discrete random variables and valid distributions","x":"A random variable X gives a number to each outcome. Discrete: countable values (0, 1, 2 …). A distribution is valid when every P(x) is between 0 and 1 and all P(x) add to 1. X = number of buses arriving at a stop in one hour. Data: illustrative. Edit the probabilities and the tool checks both conditions."},{"pt":"Week 6","l":"week-06.html#expected-value","n":"Expected value E(X)","t":"Expected value E(X)","x":"The expected value E(X), or μ, is the long-run average of X: multiply each value by its probability and add. Same bus stop: 0, 1, 2 or 3 buses with probabilities 0.1, 0.3, 0.4, 0.2. Over many hours, the stop gets 1.7 buses per hour on average. E(X) need not be a possible value of X."},{"pt":"Week 6","l":"week-06.html#variance-x","n":"Variance and standard deviation of X","t":"Variance and standard deviation of X","x":"Var(X) measures how far X typically lands from E(X). SD = √Var(X), in the units of X. Same bus stop, with μ = 1.7. In a typical hour, the number of buses is about 0.9 away from the average of 1.7. Bigger SD = less predictable."},{"pt":"Week 6","l":"week-06.html#in-class","n":"In class","t":"In class","x":""},{"pt":"Week 6","l":"week-06.html#takeaways","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":""},{"pt":"Week 6","l":"week-06.html#practice","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Solve on paper first, then open the model working. Easy to hard."},{"pt":"Week 7","l":"week-07.html#bernoulli","n":"The Bernoulli distribution","t":"The Bernoulli distribution","x":"A Bernoulli trial has one trial and two outcomes: success (x = 1) with probability p, failure (x = 0) with q = 1 − p. A player takes one penalty kick and scores with p = 0.75. Data: illustrative. Over many kicks, the average of X is 0.75: the player scores 75% of the time. A binomial count adds up n of these trials."},{"pt":"Week 7","l":"week-07.html#conditions","n":"Is it binomial? The four conditions","t":"Is it binomial? The four conditions","x":"X is binomial when: (1) the number of trials n is fixed; (2) each trial has two outcomes; (3) p is the same every trial; (4) the trials are independent. Decide for each situation. If one condition fails, the binomial formula does not apply. Check all four before calculating."},{"pt":"Week 7","l":"week-07.html#binomial-formula","n":"The binomial formula","t":"The binomial formula","x":"For a binomial X, the probability of exactly x successes in n trials uses the number of ways to place the successes, nCx. A basketball player makes 70% of her free throws. She takes 5 shots. What is P(exactly 3 made)? Data: illustrative. Each bar is one P(X = x). The bars add up to 1. With p = 0.7 the bars lean to the right: most shots are made."},{"pt":"Week 7","l":"week-07.html#at-least","n":"At least one: the complement","t":"At least one: the complement","x":"“At least one” = 1 − P(none). “At most” and “at least” add several P(X = x) values, or use the complement. Each of 6 flights is delayed with p = 0.2, independently. What is P(at least one delayed)? Data: illustrative. About 74% of such days have at least one delay. Move n, p and x below to see P(X ≤ x) and P(X ≥ x)."},{"pt":"Week 7","l":"week-07.html#mean-sd","n":"Mean and standard deviation of a binomial","t":"Mean and standard deviation of a binomial","x":"For a binomial X: mean μ = np, variance Var(X) = npq, and SD = √(npq). 15% of customers at a shop pay in cash. Out of 40 customers, how many pay in cash on average? Data: illustrative. On average 6 of the 40 customers pay in cash, usually within about 2.26 of 6 (shaded band: 3.74 to 8.26)."},{"pt":"Week 7","l":"week-07.html#in-class","n":"In class","t":"In class","x":""},{"pt":"Week 7","l":"week-07.html#takeaways","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":""},{"pt":"Week 7","l":"week-07.html#practice","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Solve on paper first, then open the model working. Easy to hard."},{"pt":"Week 8","l":"week-08.html#density","n":"From histogram to density curve","t":"From histogram to density curve","x":"A continuous random variable can take any value in a range, decimals included. Its distribution is a smooth density curve. The total area under the curve is 1. Student heights in cm. With more data and thinner bars, the histogram turns into a smooth curve. Data: simulated. For a continuous X, probability = area under the curve between two values. P(X = one exact value) is 0, so P(X < a) and P(X ≤ a) are the same."},{"pt":"Week 8","l":"week-08.html#normal-curve","n":"The normal distribution N(μ, σ)","t":"The normal distribution N(μ, σ)","x":"The normal distribution is bell-shaped and symmetric. Mean = median = mode, at the peak. It is written X ~ N(μ, σ). Exam scores, blood pressure, reaction times and newborn weights are approximately normal: most values near the middle, few in the tails. μ moves the curve left or right. σ makes it narrow and tall (small σ) or wide and flat (large σ). The total area stays 1."},{"pt":"Week 8","l":"week-08.html#empirical-rule","n":"The 68–95–99.7 rule","t":"The 68–95–99.7 rule","x":"In a normal distribution about 68% of values lie within 1σ of μ, 95% within 2σ, and 99.7% within 3σ. Adult resting heart rate: μ = 72 bpm, σ = 6 bpm. Between which values are the middle 95%? Data: illustrative. About 95% of adults have a resting heart rate between 60 and 84 bpm. Pick a range below."},{"pt":"Week 8","l":"week-08.html#rule-areas","n":"Areas from the rule","t":"Areas from the rule","x":"The curve is symmetric, so each piece outside a band is split equally between the two tails. Area = percentage = probability. Same heart rates, μ = 72, σ = 6. What percentage of adults are above 78 bpm (μ + 1σ)? Add the pieces: 50% on each side of μ, then 34%, 13.5%, 2.35% and 0.15% moving out. Above 78: 13.5 + 2.35 + 0.15 = 16%."},{"pt":"Week 8","l":"week-08.html#in-class","n":"In class","t":"In class","x":""},{"pt":"Week 8","l":"week-08.html#takeaways","n":"Takeaways","t":"Takeaways","x":""},{"pt":"Week 8","l":"week-08.html#practice","n":"Practice","t":"Practice","x":"Solve on paper first, then open the model working. Easy to hard."},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#part-1","n":"Part 1 · Graphs and skewness","t":"Part 1 · Graphs and skewness","x":"Read the values from the graph first, then calculate. For the shape, follow the long tail. 1ASkewness: which way? Follow the long tail. The tail is the side where the graph goes down slowly and stretches out far. The tail gives the name.\n\nFind the peak: the highest bar or point.\nFind the side where the graph goes down slowly into a long tail.\nTail on the RIGHT → right-skewed (positive). Tail on the LEFT → left-skewed (negative). No tail, both sides the same → symmetric.\n\nCheck with the numbers. The mean is pulled toward the tail. Right-skewed: mean > median. Left-skewed: mean < median. Symmetric: mean ≈ median.\n\nCareful. “It drops toward the right” is right-skewed only when the graph goes do"},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#part-2","n":"Part 2 · Calculations","t":"Part 2 · Calculations","x":"Write the formula, then the numbers. Sort the data before the median and the quartiles. 2ACentral tendency 2BMeasures of variation The campus shuttle. Nine students record how many minutes they waited for the shuttle this morning: 12, 8, 24, 9, 6, 16, 8, 14, 11.\nFind the mean.\nSort the data and find the median.\nFind the mode.\nFind the range, Q1, Q3 and IQR.\nUse the fences Q1 − 1.5 × IQR and Q3 + 1.5 × IQR. Is 24 an outlier?\nFind the variance (Var = s²) and the standard deviation (SD = s).\nFind the CV.\nCompare the mean and the median. Which way is the data skewed? Which centre would you report?"},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#part-3","n":"Part 3 · Probability","t":"Part 3 · Probability","x":"One set for all the rules. Find the key word first (and, or, not), then choose the rule from the table. 3AProbability rules: mixed 3BTree diagrams and sample space The campus café meal deal. A customer chooses at random a main (Burger or Wrap), then a drink (Juice or Water), then whether to add a dessert (Yes or No). Every choice is equally likely.\nDraw the tree diagram.\nList the sample space. How many outcomes are there?\nP(Wrap and Juice and dessert)?\nP(Juice)?\nP(Burger or Water)?\nP(no dessert)?\nP(not the meal Burger, Juice, Yes)?\nLast week the café also counted 60 customers: 36 bought coffee, 24 bought cake and 15 bought both.\nFill the numbers in the diagram below.\nCoffee\nCake\n?\n?\n?\n?\nP(co"},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#part-4","n":"Part 4 · Discrete distributions","t":"Part 4 · Discrete distributions","x":"Every P(x) is between 0 and 1, and the sum is 1. E(X) = Σx·P(x). Var(X) = Σ(x − μ)²·P(x). 4AValid distribution and k 4BE(X), Var(X) and SD A food delivery app. X = the number of late deliveries a rider has in one day.\nX\t0\t1\t2\t3\nP(x)\t0.2\t0.3\tk\t0.1\nIs X discrete or continuous? Why?\nFind k.\nFind P(X ≥ 2) and P(at least one late delivery).\nFind P(X = 0 or X = 3).\nFind E(X) and interpret it.\nFind Var(X) and SD.\nAbout how many late deliveries should the rider expect in 30 days?\nDays are independent. P(no late delivery on both of two days)?"},{"pt":"Midterm revision","l":"midterm-practice.html#exam-day","n":"Exam day","t":"Exam day","x":""}];
